The vectors are coplanar if and only if their scalar triple product is zero. Expanding along the first row gives

$$
\begin{aligned}
\mathbf{a}\cdot(\mathbf{b}\times\mathbf{c})&=\begin{vmatrix}-2&-1&1\\-2&-2&-1\\-3&2&k\end{vmatrix}\\&=(-2)\cdot \begin{vmatrix}-2&-1\\2&k\end{vmatrix}\\&\quad -(-1)\cdot \begin{vmatrix}-2&-1\\-3&k\end{vmatrix}\\&\quad +(1)\cdot \begin{vmatrix}-2&-2\\-3&2\end{vmatrix}\\&=(-2)\cdot ((-2)\cdot (k)-(-1)\cdot (2))\\&\quad -(-1)\cdot ((-2)\cdot (k)-(-1)\cdot (-3))\\&\quad +(1)\cdot ((-2)\cdot (2)-(-2)\cdot (-3))\\&=(-2)\cdot (2 - 2 k)\\&\quad -(-1)\cdot (- 2 k - 3)\\&\quad +(1)\cdot (-10)\\&=2 k - 17
\end{aligned}
$$

Set the triple product equal to zero and solve:

$$
\begin{aligned}
2 k - 17&=0\\2 k&=17\\k&=\frac{17}{2}
\end{aligned}
$$

Thus the vectors are coplanar exactly when $k=\frac{17}{2}$.

The vectors are coplanar if and only if their scalar triple product is zero. Expanding along the first row gives

$$
\begin{aligned}
\mathbf{a}\cdot(\mathbf{b}\times\mathbf{c})&=\begin{vmatrix}1&-2&k\\-3&2&-1\\-3&1&k\end{vmatrix}\\&=(1)\cdot \begin{vmatrix}2&-1\\1&k\end{vmatrix}\\&\quad -(-2)\cdot \begin{vmatrix}-3&-1\\-3&k\end{vmatrix}\\&\quad +(k)\cdot \begin{vmatrix}-3&2\\-3&1\end{vmatrix}\\&=(1)\cdot ((2)\cdot (k)-(-1)\cdot (1))\\&\quad -(-2)\cdot ((-3)\cdot (k)-(-1)\cdot (-3))\\&\quad +(k)\cdot ((-3)\cdot (1)-(2)\cdot (-3))\\&=(1)\cdot (2 k + 1)\\&\quad -(-2)\cdot (- 3 k - 3)\\&\quad +(k)\cdot (3)\\&=- k - 5
\end{aligned}
$$

Set the triple product equal to zero and solve:

$$
\begin{aligned}
- k - 5&=0\\- k&=5\\k&=-5
\end{aligned}
$$

Thus the vectors are coplanar exactly when $k=-5$.

The vectors are coplanar if and only if their scalar triple product is zero. Expanding along the first row gives

$$
\begin{aligned}
\mathbf{a}\cdot(\mathbf{b}\times\mathbf{c})&=\begin{vmatrix}-1&3&2\\-2&k&2\\3&2&-2\end{vmatrix}\\&=(-1)\cdot \begin{vmatrix}k&2\\2&-2\end{vmatrix}\\&\quad -(3)\cdot \begin{vmatrix}-2&2\\3&-2\end{vmatrix}\\&\quad +(2)\cdot \begin{vmatrix}-2&k\\3&2\end{vmatrix}\\&=(-1)\cdot ((k)\cdot (-2)-(2)\cdot (2))\\&\quad -(3)\cdot ((-2)\cdot (-2)-(2)\cdot (3))\\&\quad +(2)\cdot ((-2)\cdot (2)-(k)\cdot (3))\\&=(-1)\cdot (- 2 k - 4)\\&\quad -(3)\cdot (-2)\\&\quad +(2)\cdot (- 3 k - 4)\\&=2 - 4 k
\end{aligned}
$$

Set the triple product equal to zero and solve:

$$
\begin{aligned}
2 - 4 k&=0\\- 4 k&=-2\\k&=\frac{1}{2}
\end{aligned}
$$

Thus the vectors are coplanar exactly when $k=\frac{1}{2}$.
